CAT = "ptaCardio"; SEG = "CAR"
Q = [
("A patient in phase I cardiac rehab after an uncomplicated MI walks in the hallway. Which heart rate response is generally acceptable?",
 "A rise of up to 20 bpm over rest", ["A rise to 85% of age-predicted max", "A fall of 10 bpm below rest", "A rise of 40 bpm over rest"],
 "Inpatient cardiac rehab usually keeps the heart rate within about 20 bpm of resting (30 bpm after surgery). A fall in heart rate with more work is a sign to stop, not a goal."),
("A patient takes a beta blocker for hypertension. How should the PTA best monitor exercise intensity?",
 "Rating of perceived exertion", ["Age-predicted target heart rate", "Resting respiratory rate only", "Pulse oximetry alone"],
 "Beta blockers blunt the heart rate response to exercise, so a target heart rate from a formula is unreliable. RPE (with blood pressure and symptoms) tracks effort instead."),
("A patient's systolic pressure falls 15 mmHg as treadmill speed is raised. What should the PTA do?",
 "Stop and report it", ["Raise the speed to recheck", "Continue at the same speed", "Add arm work to the session"],
 "A drop in systolic pressure as workload rises suggests the heart cannot meet demand. Exercise should stop, vitals be monitored, and the PT notified."),
("On standing, a patient's blood pressure goes from 128/78 to 104/70 with dizziness. What is this?",
 "Orthostatic hypotension", ["Exercise-induced hypertension", "Autonomic dysreflexia", "Normal postural response"],
 "A fall of at least 20 mmHg systolic or 10 mmHg diastolic within a few minutes of standing, often with dizziness, is orthostatic hypotension. Return the patient to sitting or supine."),
("Six weeks after a median sternotomy for CABG, which activity is still commonly restricted?",
 "Pushing up with both arms", ["Walking on level ground", "Ankle pumps while seated", "Diaphragmatic breathing"],
 "Sternal precautions limit pushing, pulling and lifting with the arms while the sternum heals. Exact limits and duration follow the surgeon's protocol."),
("Why does a patient with COPD use pursed-lip breathing during exertion?",
 "To keep airways open longer", ["To raise the respiratory rate", "To increase CO2 retention", "To shorten each exhalation"],
 "Pursed lips create back pressure that splints small airways open, slows the breathing rate and helps empty trapped air. Exhalation becomes longer, not shorter."),
("While teaching diaphragmatic breathing, where should the PTA see the most movement during inhalation?",
 "The upper abdomen rising", ["The upper chest rising", "The shoulders elevating", "The neck muscles tightening"],
 "In diaphragmatic breathing the diaphragm descends and the upper abdomen moves outward. Upper chest and shoulder movement shows accessory muscle use."),
("Which position drains the posterior basal segments of both lower lobes?",
 "Prone, foot of bed raised", ["Sitting upright, leaning back", "Supine with the bed flat", "Side-lying with the head raised"],
 "The posterior basal segments drain prone with the head down (foot of the bed raised about 20 inches). Upright sitting drains the apical upper lobe segments."),
("A patient is scheduled for chest percussion over the right lower lobe. Which finding should make the PTA hold it?",
 "A rib fracture there", ["Thick mucus on auscultation", "A productive morning cough", "Mild shortness of breath"],
 "Percussion is avoided over a fracture, a fresh incision or other unstable chest wall. Thick secretions are the reason for airway clearance, not a reason to stop."),
("What does a huff cough do that a strong closed-glottis cough does not?",
 "Limits early airway collapse", ["Raises intrathoracic pressure more", "Clears only the upper airway", "Needs a mechanical device"],
 "Huffing is a forced exhalation with an open glottis. It moves mucus from smaller airways with less pressure, so unstable airways are less likely to collapse."),
("After abdominal surgery a patient is given an incentive spirometer. What is its main purpose?",
 "Prevent atelectasis", ["Strengthen expiratory muscles", "Measure peak expiratory flow", "Deliver inhaled medication"],
 "A slow, sustained deep breath held at the top inflates the alveoli and helps prevent collapse after surgery. Peak flow is measured with a different device."),
("Which finding most suggests right-sided heart failure?",
 "Pitting edema of both ankles", ["Crackles at both lung bases", "Shortness of breath lying flat", "Frothy pink sputum"],
 "Right-sided failure backs blood into the systemic veins, causing peripheral edema and jugular venous distension. Crackles, orthopnea and frothy sputum point to the left side."),
("A patient with peripheral arterial disease begins a walking program. How is it usually done?",
 "Walk to moderate pain, rest", ["Stop at the first sign of discomfort", "Walk through severe pain each bout", "Use only seated arm exercise"],
 "Intermittent claudication programs have the patient walk until moderate pain, rest until it eases, then repeat. Over weeks, pain-free walking distance increases."),
("Which ankle-brachial index reading suggests peripheral arterial disease?",
 "0.7", ["1.1", "1.0", "1.5"],
 "An ABI below 0.9 suggests arterial disease. About 1.0 to 1.4 is normal, and above 1.4 suggests stiff, noncompressible vessels, common with diabetes."),
("During gait training a patient reports new calf pain, and the calf is warm and swollen. What should the PTA do?",
 "Stop and notify nurse and PT", ["Massage the calf to ease it", "Have the patient walk it off", "Apply a hot pack to the calf"],
 "These signs suggest deep vein thrombosis. Activity stops, the limb is not massaged, and the medical team is told at once because of the risk of pulmonary embolism."),
("A patient on warfarin trips in the gym and bumps her head but says she feels fine. What should the PTA do?",
 "Report it for medical review", ["Continue if she feels well", "Apply ice and carry on", "Recheck at the next visit"],
 "Anticoagulants raise the risk of bleeding inside the skull, which may show no signs at first. A fall with a head strike is reported at once for medical review."),
("A patient with COPD on 2 L/min of oxygen walks and her SpO2 falls to 85%. What should the PTA do first?",
 "Stop and let her rest", ["Raise the flow to 6 L/min", "Remove the cannula to recheck", "Walk faster to finish sooner"],
 "Saturation below about 88% during activity calls for rest and recovery, then reporting to the PT. Oxygen is ordered, so the flow is not changed without an order."),
("Which breathing advice fits a patient with hypertension doing resistance exercise?",
 "Exhale during the lift", ["Hold the breath during the lift", "Inhale during the lift", "Breathe only through the nose"],
 "Breath holding causes a Valsalva maneuver, which sharply raises blood pressure. Exhaling during the effort phase avoids it."),
("Before exercise, a patient's resting blood pressure is 212/118. What should the PTA do?",
 "Hold it and notify the PT", ["Start with a longer warm-up", "Proceed with light resistance", "Recheck after the session"],
 "Resting pressures above about 200 systolic or 110 diastolic are a usual reason not to begin exercise. The PTA holds the session and reports it."),
("A patient took sublingual nitroglycerin 10 minutes ago. What should the PTA watch for when standing him up?",
 "Dizziness from low pressure", ["A sharp rise in pressure", "A slowing of the heart rate", "Wheezing and chest tightness"],
 "Nitroglycerin dilates blood vessels, so blood pressure can fall and cause dizziness or fainting on standing. Rise slowly and monitor."),
("Which position usually eases dyspnea for a patient with severe COPD?",
 "Leaning forward, arms supported", ["Lying flat with no pillow", "Standing with both arms overhead", "Side-lying with the head down"],
 "Leaning forward with the arms supported fixes the shoulder girdle for the accessory muscles and lets the abdomen push the diaphragm into a better position."),
("Spirometry shows reduced total lung capacity with a normal FEV1/FVC ratio. What pattern is this?",
 "Restrictive", ["Obstructive", "Mixed obstructive", "Normal for age"],
 "Restrictive disease (such as pulmonary fibrosis) shrinks lung volumes. Obstructive disease lowers the FEV1/FVC ratio because air leaves the lungs slowly."),
("A teenager with cystic fibrosis wants to do airway clearance at home without help. Which option fits?",
 "An oscillating PEP device", ["Postural drainage by a parent", "A nebulized bronchodilator only", "A cough suppressant"],
 "Oscillating positive expiratory pressure devices let a patient loosen and move mucus on their own. Suppressing the cough would keep secretions in the lungs."),
("What does the 6-minute walk test mainly record?",
 "Distance walked", ["Peak heart rate", "Number of rests", "Maximal oxygen uptake"],
 "The test measures how far the patient walks in 6 minutes, a measure of functional exercise capacity. Rests are allowed and vital signs are noted alongside."),
("A 58-year-old has a resting heart rate of 74. Using the heart rate reserve method, what is 50% intensity?",
 "118 bpm", ["81 bpm", "44 bpm", "155 bpm"],
 "Max is 220 - 58 = 162; reserve is 162 - 74 = 88; 50% of 88 is 44, plus 74 rest = 118 bpm. 81 is 50% of max without the reserve step."),
("A patient had a heart transplant a year ago. How does the heart respond to the start of exercise?",
 "The rate rises slowly", ["The rate rises at once", "The rate does not change", "The rate falls at first"],
 "The transplanted heart is denervated, so rate rises only as circulating catecholamines increase. Longer warm-ups and cool-downs and RPE are used."),
("A patient on a cardiac monitor shows a new irregular rhythm with dizziness during exercise. What should the PTA do?",
 "Stop and report it at once", ["Continue but slow the pace", "Take a radial pulse later", "Ask about it next session"],
 "New arrhythmias with symptoms are a reason to stop exercise and notify the PT and medical team right away."),
("A patient with heart failure gained 1.5 kg since yesterday and has more ankle swelling. What does this suggest?",
 "Fluid retention", ["Muscle gain from training", "Improved cardiac output", "Normal daily variation"],
 "A rapid gain of more than about 1 to 1.5 kg in a day, or 2 kg in a week, suggests fluid build-up from worsening heart failure. It should be reported before exercise."),
("Which breath sound is a high-pitched, musical sound heard mostly on expiration?",
 "Wheeze", ["Crackle", "Stridor", "Pleural rub"],
 "Wheezes come from narrowed airways, as in asthma. Crackles are discontinuous popping sounds; stridor is high-pitched and heard mostly on inspiration over the upper airway."),
("What is the usual first sign that a patient's aerobic exercise is too hard?",
 "Cannot finish a sentence", ["A slight rise in breathing rate", "Mild sweating", "A warm feeling in the legs"],
 "The talk test is a simple guide: at moderate intensity a patient can talk but not sing. Being unable to finish a sentence suggests the effort is too high."),
("A deconditioned older adult starts a walking program. How should it usually be progressed first?",
 "Duration before intensity", ["Speed before duration", "Add hills in week one", "Double the distance each day"],
 "Building up time at a comfortable pace comes first; intensity is raised once the patient tolerates longer bouts."),
("A patient with a chest tube is being walked. Where should the drainage system stay?",
 "Below chest level", ["Above the shoulder", "On the patient's lap", "Clamped during the walk"],
 "The collection unit stays upright and below the chest so drainage cannot flow back. Chest tubes are not clamped for mobility unless ordered."),
]
